BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20261015T234806Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53560131929269
DTSTART:20261020T143000Z
DTEND:20261020T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:Do you still do citations by hand? Invest an hour and a half of your academic career to learn how to utilize a free citation management program and save yourself countless hours in the future. In this workshop\, we will demonstrate how to use Zotero to collect\, save\, and organize citations and create citations and bibliographies with just a few clicks. Zotero is compatible with Microsoft Word and Google Docs\, allowing you to link your sources directly to your writing. With just a few additional clicks\, you can easily modify the style of your bibliography. The workshop is tailored to accommodate beginners and those with some Zotero experience. Participants interested in attending Systematic Review workshops are encouraged to take this workshop first.\n\n \n\nPlease bring your laptop to the session. If you don’t have one\, you can check one out at the Scholar Support Desk.\n\n \n\nPlease contact this QEP's instructor\, Jonathan DaSo\, if you have questions about workshop content.  \n\n \n\nPlease arrive at the workshop at least 5 minutes before the start time. Please have your FSU Card ready for check-in. Check-in ends 10 minutes after the scheduled start time and late arrivals will not be admitted. \n\n \n\nThe Graduate Skills Workshop series is a collection of workshops developed in collaboration with the Graduate School and the Quality Enhancement Plan (QEP) to support graduate research and success. \n  \n\nPlease note: The target audience for this workshop is graduate and doctoral students\, but participants at all levels are welcome to join.  To qualify for the Research and Creative Activity Travel Grant\, only fully matriculated and admitted FSU students will be considered.\n\n \n\nFor questions regarding Research and Creative Activity Travel Grants\, please contact the Graduate Student Resource Center.
GEO:30.44502;-84.299845
LOCATION:Dirac Science Library (DSL)\, 208 (Computer Lab/Instruction Room)
SUMMARY:Introduction to Citation Management Using Zotero
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.fsu.edu/event/Fall26-Zotero-Oct20
CATEGORIES:Professional Development
CATEGORIES:Workshops & Seminars
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20261015T234806Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53578473727937
DTSTART:20261020T190000Z
DTEND:20261020T203000Z
DESCRIPTION:Our Introduction to Parallel R workshop is a free\, interactive session that provides a practical introduction to parallel computing in the R language\, with a focus on accelerating data processing and statistical analysis.\nThis workshop is designed for researchers in STEM and the Humanities who already have experience programming in R and are seeking to optimize their workflows for large datasets.\n\nTopics Covered\nBy the end of this workshop\, attendees will be able to connect to the High-Performance Compute cluster and use R’s built-in parallel computing package to improve the performance of their research tasks. Attendees will also gain a foundational understanding of parallel computing concepts that can be applied across programming languages.\n\nSpecific topics include:\n\nOverview of Parallel Computing\n\nHow to Identify Tasks Suitable for Parallelization· Connecting to RCC Systems\n\nIntroduction to the 'parallel' Package in R\n\nParallelizing Data Processing for Large Datasets\n\nRunning Parallel Regressions for Large Datasets\n\nFor online attendance: \n\nYou MUST use your FSU email account to access this workshop through Zoom. Please ensure that your Zoom app is up to date before the workshop.  \n\nFor in-person attendance:\n\nPlease have your FSU Card ready for check-in.
GEO:30.44502;-84.299845
LOCATION:Dirac Science Library (DSL)\, Computer Lab\, 208
SUMMARY:Data at Your Desk Series: Parallel R
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.fsu.edu/event/F26-DAYD-ParallelR-Oct20
CATEGORIES:Professional Development
CATEGORIES:Workshops & Seminars
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20261015T234806Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53560398048841
DTSTART:20261021T180000Z
DTEND:20261021T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:Formerly known as the Literature in Natural Sciences\, this updated session is designed for graduate students in biology\, geology\, atmospheric sciences\, oceanography\, environmental sciences\, and other earth‑science–related programs.\n\nConducting a comprehensive literature review is essential for identifying research problems and questions that advance your thesis or dissertation. This session will cover strategies and best practices for searching scholarly resources\, organizing information\, identifying relevant studies to support your research\, and other helpful search tips and tricks.\n\nWe’ll discuss how to select the right databases for your topic\, how to write and track your searches\, and how to use tools and features within each database. The session will focus on effectively searching Web of Science and Scopus\, with additional databases included as time and attendee interest allow.\n\nWe’ll work through foundational strategies for searching scientific literature to develop strong research questions and move your work forward. Bring your research topics and questions!\n\n \n\nPlease contact this QEP's instructors\, Kelly Grove or Kassidy Hof-Mahoney\, if you have questions about workshop content.  \n\n \n\nYou MUST use your FSU email account to access this workshop through Zoom. Please ensure that your Zoom app is up to date before the workshop.  Check-in ends 10 minutes after the scheduled start time and late arrivals will not be admitted. \n\n \n\nThe Graduate Skills Workshop series is a collection of workshops developed in collaboration with the Graduate School and the Quality Enhancement Plan (QEP) to support graduate research and success. \n  \n\nPlease note: The target audience for this workshop is graduate and doctoral students\, but participants at all levels are welcome to join.  To qualify for the Research and Creative Activity Travel Grant\, only fully matriculated and admitted FSU students will be considered.\n\n \n\nFor questions regarding Research and Creative Activity Travel Grants\, please contact the Graduate Student Resource Center.
GEO:30.44502;-84.299845
LOCATION:Dirac Science Library (DSL)\, 208
SUMMARY:Web of Science and Scopus for Graduate Students
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.fsu.edu/event/Fall26-WebofScience-Scopus-Oct21
CATEGORIES:Professional Development
CATEGORIES:Workshops & Seminars
END:VEVENT
